*Application for Lecturer in Sustainable Engineering, Reference EPSME1208*

I am writing to apply for the post of *Lecturer in Sustainable Engineering* in the School of Mechanical Engineering. My research field is the multi-physics design, thermal management and AI-assisted modelling of electrified powertrains, which sits directly on the post's focus on *sustainable engineering with regard to automotive engineering applications*. I am currently a Research Assistant at the Power Electronics and Machines Centre (PEMC), University of Nottingham, having completed my PhD at Southeast University (SEU), Nanjing, in September 2025 with the dissertation _Multi-physics Analysis and Design of Hairpin Winding PM Traction Machines in Electric Vehicles_. The PhD is in Electrical Engineering, a closely related discipline, and its substance is as much mechanical as electrical: lumped-parameter thermal networks and fluid-solid coupled oil-cooling models, mechanical stress and structural-modal analysis of high-speed rotors, and manufacturing-tolerance design, all validated on AVL dynamometers.

My research record includes *22 journal papers (17 in IEEE Transactions)*, ten as first author, 18 peer-reviewed conference papers and 9 patents, recognised by the *2025 IEEE TEC Electric Vehicle Design Competition (1#super[st] Prize)* and the *2025 IEEE Transactions on Transportation Electrification Prize Paper Award (2#super[nd] Prize)*. Through a multi-year collaboration with Inovance, a world-leading EV traction-machine supplier, I delivered two high-speed oil-cooled PM traction machines for A0-class and A-class vehicles (18,000 rpm, 113 kW; 20,000 rpm, 270 kW) through the full industrial cycle from multi-physics design and engineering-drawing release to dynamometer characterisation.

The post asks for a coherent and ambitious research vision in sustainable engineering. Mine is a programme on *AI-driven design and digital twins of electrified powertrains* for net-zero road transport, built from three connected strands: multi-physics modelling of automotive traction machines and their converters; *deep-learning surrogate models* that replace expensive finite-element evaluations, where my ICEM 2026 paper reaches R#super[2] = 0.9996 for torque prediction with more than 94% inference speed-up, built on an in-house Python finite-element code package; and experimental validation on dynamometer-tested industrial prototypes. The programme serves sustainability twice over, through vehicles that consume less energy in operation and through magnet-free and reduced-rare-earth machine topologies that lower material criticality, the focus of my current work on the multi-institution AIMD programme (Nottingham, Bristol, Newcastle). I would pursue an *EPSRC New Investigator Award* within the first eighteen months, Innovate UK and *Advanced Propulsion Centre* industrially co-funded projects building on my prototype delivery record, and participation in larger consortium bids as the group grows. Within the School I see natural links to the *Institute of Thermofluids* on thermal management of electrified powertrains and to the *Institute of Design, Robotics and Manufacturing* on design optimisation and electric drives as actuation.

On teaching, I served as teaching assistant on the SEU postgraduate course _Integrated Practice of Electrical Machine Systems_, covering tutorial delivery, daily consultation hours, structured written feedback on student project proposals and mark recommendations. I have research-derived materials ready to develop into *research-led teaching* that maps onto the School's programmes, including the *Automotive Engineering MEng/BEng* with its coverage of electric and hybrid vehicle technology and modern drivetrain technology: dynamometer data from two industrial automotive prototypes as worked examples, the Python finite-element package for simulation-based design coursework and a digital-twin exercise, and embedded motor-drive laboratory practicals on TI C2000 and STM32 platforms supporting mechatronics, dynamics and control. I would be glad to support Leeds Gryphon Racing on the electric powertrain side, would act as a personal tutor, and would work toward *Fellowship of the Higher Education Academy* as an early priority.

Having completed my BEng, MSc and PhD at Southeast University, I know the Chinese university system from the inside and am a native Mandarin speaker, so I am well placed to support the School's *transnational education* activities, including overseas teaching of up to six weeks per academic year. I have studied and worked across distinctly different institutional cultures in China and the United Kingdom, and I am committed to inclusive teaching, supervision and mentoring practice and to the wider academic citizenship of the School.

My academic CV, including a full publication list, is attached, and my responses to the selection criteria are provided in the supporting statement section of the online application. I would be glad to discuss my application further at interview.

Yours sincerely,

